---
import { Code } from "astro:components"
import Layout from "../components/Layout.astro"

const code = `
plugin("@boring/blurs"):mount({
  radius = 16,
})

plugin("@boring/shadows"):mount({
  radius = 4,
  color = "#000000",
})

plugin("@boring/animations"):mount({
  window = {
    -- Animation syntax is roughly similar to CSS
    -- Window dimensions and positions
    open = "100ms ease-out",
    close = "100ms ease-out",
    move = "100ms ease-out",
    resize = "100ms ease-out",

    -- Border color changes
    border_color = "100ms ease-out",

    -- You can also define bezier curves with cubic-bezier(x1, y1, x2, y2)
    -- open = "100ms cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1)",
  }
})
`
---

<Layout title="Questions & Answers">
  <section>
    <h1>Questions & Answers</h1>
    <p>
      <h2>Why is it called <i>boring</i>?</h2>
      <p>
        Because it is. Boring software is good software. Pursuing an
        ever-increasing scope of features doesn't necessarily make software
        better.
      </p>
      <h2>Is <i>boring</i> tiling, stacking, or floating?</h2>
      <p>
        The window management strategy is up to you. You can make your own with
        a plugin, and it isn't terribly complicated. However, for the lazy in
        you, we maintain three official plugins for window management:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <code>layout-stacking</code>, which provides a traditional desktop
          layout
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>layout-tiling</code>, which is inspired by Sway's n-ary trees
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>layout-kiosk</code>, which displays a single, full-screen window
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
    <h2>How much eye-candy is there?</h2>
    <p>
      By default, <i>boring</i> has very little eye-candy. However, its renderer is
      based on GLES2, which allows for much of the effects Wayland users have come
      to expect from their compositors, such as blurs, shadows, and animations. We
      have reference implementations for each of these in the
      <code>blurs</code>, <code>shadows</code>, and <code>animations</code> official
      plugins, which can be enabled by appending the following to your configuration
      file:
    </p>
    <Code code={code.trim()} lang="lua" theme="github-light-high-contrast" />
  </section>
</Layout>
